<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tufa Wugu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 399</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tufa Wugu was the founder of Southern Liang, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Chief of the Tufa branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>, settled in the Huangshui valley of eastern Qinghai, he broke away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a> in 397 and proclaimed himself king. He died in 399 after a fall from his horse and was succeeded by his brothers Tufa Lilugu and Tufa Rutan, who continued to rule the state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tufa Wugu's father, Tufa Sifujian, was a great-grandnephew of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> general Tufa Shujineng, who had troubled the Jin dynasty under Emperor Wu. Sifujian became tribal chief in 356 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Former Qin; when he died and was succeeded by his son is not known. Tufa Wugu was described as brave and ambitious and set his sights on Liang Province. On the advice of his general Fen Tuo he encouraged agriculture and tried to rule efficiently and fairly.</p><p>In 394 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-guang">Lü Guang</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, offered him a generalship. Most of his advisers thought it humiliating to become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, but the strategist Shizhen Ruoliu argued that he was not yet strong enough to oppose Lü Guang and should submit so as to make him arrogant, and Tufa Wugu accepted. In 395 he forced neighbouring tribes, among them the Yifu and Zhejue, to submit, built Lianchuan Castle as his headquarters and was made Duke of Guangwu by Lü Guang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-guang">Lü Guang</a> took the title Heavenly King in 396 and offered him further honours, Tufa Wugu refused them, denouncing Lü Guang's sons and nephews as corrupt and cruel, though he kept the musicians and artisans sent with the titles. In 397, after Lü Guang was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qifu-gangui">Qifu Gangui</a> of Western Qin, he claimed a regal title of his own, changed the era name and took the city of Jincheng, then defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a> general Dou Gou.</p><p>His aim was to weaken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a> and take its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a>. In 398 the Later Liang generals Yang Gui and Wang Qiji went over to him, and after he defeated the Qiang chief Liang Ji the Qiang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> tribes south of the Hongchi Mountain submitted. Late that year he changed his title to Prince of Wuwei, perhaps a sign of his designs on Guzang. In spring 399 he moved his capital to Ledu, and he was remembered for placing Han and other subjects alike according to their talent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Later in 399 he helped the Northern Liang prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-ye">Duan Ye</a> fend off an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-guang">Lü Guang</a>'s sons Lü Shao and Lü Zuan. Soon afterwards he fell from his horse while drunk and suffered a severe chest injury, remarking that he was making Lü Guang and his sons happy. As he grew worse he ordered that an older man should succeed him, and the nobles chose his brother Tufa Lilugu. His posthumous title was Prince Wu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tufa Wugu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 397 their chief <b>Tufa Wugu</b> rebelled against Later Liang and proclaimed himself king; the capital became Ledu in eastern Qinghai.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-liang">Southern Liang</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39,100&amp;year=389" aria-label="Hexi Corridor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tufa Wugu"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5661 4205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5661 4205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39,100&amp;year=389">What was here in 389 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
